#!/usr/bin/env python3

#panzer_planner_server — shared backend for the Student Planner (runs on Linux, incl. IBM LinuxONE / s390x)
#====================================================================================================

#What it does
#------------
# Stores each student's calendar feed URLs (iCal / webcal) and downloads them on a schedule,
  #so timetables stay fresh even when nobody has the desktop app open.
# Lets students join a *group* and compare availability. Other members only ever receive
 # "busy" time ranges: never the calendar URLs, never the class titles or locations.
# Exposes a small JSON REST API that the tkinter app uses in "Server mode".

#Requirements: Python 3.9+ and the standard library only.
#Optional:     `pip install cryptography` to encrypt calendar URLs/tokens in the database.

#Quick start
#-----------
#python panzer_planner_server adduser alice          # prints alice's API token (shown once)
#python panzer_planner_server adduser sam
#PLANNER_TZ=Australia/Melbourne python panzer_planner_server serve --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8765

#Configuration (environment variables)
#-------------------------------------
#    PLANNER_DB             SQLite file                       (default ./planner.db)
#    PLANNER_TZ             Time zone all times are shown in  (default: the server's own zone)
#    PLANNER_SYNC_MINUTES   Background sync interval          (default 30; 0 disables)
#    PLANNER_SECRET_KEY     Fernet key; enables encryption of stored URLs/headers
#                           (generate:  python -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet;
#                                       print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())")
#    PLANNER_TRUST_PROXY    "1" when behind ONE reverse proxy: rate-limits by X-Forwarded-For instead
#                           of the proxy's address (never set this if clients can reach the port directly)
#    PLANNER_ALLOW_PRIVATE  "1" lets the server fetch feeds from private/loopback addresses
#                           (off by default: blocks requests to internal networks)

#Put it behind a TLS reverse proxy (nginx, Caddy, HAProxy) or pass --certfile/--keyfile.
#Tokens are sent on every request, so do not expose the plain-HTTP port to an untrusted network.


